<title>CHILE: Chile's Quebrada Blanca pit starts pay talks.</title>
<headline>Chile's Quebrada Blanca pit starts pay talks.</headline>
<dateline>SANTIAGO 1997-03-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Unions at Canadian-owned Quebrada Blanca copper mine are due to present their pay demands to management Tuesday, kicking off scheduled collective wage talks at the mine in northern Chile.</p>
<p>The company has around two weeks to reply to the proposals, said general manager James Drake, adding, &quot;We are likely to reply earlier.&quot;</p>
<p>The collective contract with the pit's 500 workers will expire at the end of April.  </p>
<p>Vancouver-based Cominco Ltd Cominco Resources and Teck Resources International hold a 76.5 percent stake in Quebrada Blanca which lies high in the Andes near the border with Bolivia.</p>
<p>The two-year old pit is designed to produce 75,000 tonnes of copper cathodes a year.</p>
<p>((-- Margaret Orgill, Santiago newsroom, 562-699-5595x212))</p>
